# Live Caddy site for serving the BAM Store repository over the tailnet.
#
# This is managed through the `caddyedit` Record API (see the `caddy` skill), not
# hand-edited — the block below documents what that record installs into
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile:
#
#     apps.bam:80 {
#         handle /apks/* {
#             root * /data/storage/bam-store
#             file_server
#         }
#         handle {
#             root * /data/android/store/repo
#             file_server
#         }
#     }
#
# Two roots because the store is split: index.json, icons and the committed
# changelog sidecars live in the git checkout, while the APK binaries (hundreds
# of MB) live on the big disk under directories.bam-store-apks from
# config.yaml. The client sees one flat repo either way — `apks/<file>` URLs
# resolve through the first handle.
#
# Plain HTTP is intentional: everything is reached over Tailscale, which already
# encrypts and authenticates the connection, so TLS would just re-wrap an
# encrypted tunnel. The client's default base URL is http://apps.bam/.
#
# No bearer token is set — access is limited to the tailnet by Tailscale ACLs. If
# you later want per-app gating, add a matcher that checks
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and have the client send the same token.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The F-Droid-style repo (same box, its own hostname) — see README.md →
# "Serving the repo". Added through the same caddyedit Record API; its root
# must also be bind-mounted into /data/caddy-docker/docker-compose.yml.
#
#     fdroid.bam:80 {
#         root * /data/storage/fdroid      # directories.fdroid-repo
#         file_server browse
#     }
#
# Clients add http://fdroid.bam/repo — that string is also `fdroid.url` in
# config.yaml and is baked into the signed index, so the two must match.
